# gregOS: /snap -> /var/lib/snapd/snap
#
# Classic-confined snaps (e.g. `code --classic`) require snaps to be reachable
# under /snap. snapd only accepts two layouts (dirs.SupportsClassicConfinement
# in the snapd source):
#
#   * /snap as a real directory  (snapd's own mount dir, the default on Ubuntu)
#   * /snap as a symlink to /var/lib/snapd/snap  (Fedora/Arch documented setup)
#
# Fedora's snapd package uses the second layout (/var/lib/snapd/snap) and does
# NOT ship the symlink - the user is told to create it by hand. On a mutable
# Fedora that is `sudo ln -s /var/lib/snapd/snap /snap`; on this immutable
# bootc image the root filesystem is read-only, so the link has to be baked
# into the image at build time (same trick the snapd spec uses for Amazon
# Linux: `ln -sr /var/lib/snapd/snap /snap`).
#
# /var is the persistent, writable part of an ostree system, so the target is
# always valid at runtime and the link survives every deployment.
#
# Created by build_files/build.sh ("Snap package support") rather than here:
# systemd-tmpfiles refuses to create entries whose path is a symlink to outside
# /var or /run ("Unsafe path transition"), and /snap is a top-level path.

# Managed by gregOS. See build_files/build.sh and README.md ("Snap packages").
